  "confidence": 0.42,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["J7", "J8"],
      "pins": ["J7.1", "J7.2", "J7.3", "J8.1", "J8.2", "J8.3"],
      "nets": ["+12V", "FAN_0_TACH", "FAN_1_TACH"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet, the fan headers J7 and J8 are drawn as 4-pin connectors (Conn_01x04_Pin, pin 4 marked no-connect). The FAN_0_TACH / FAN_1_TACH signals are brought to pin 2 of J7/J8, while the +12V rail is wired to a different pin of each connector (pin 1 and/or pin 3) together with the D7 reverse-clamp diode.",
      "expected": "The spec states that the 12 V pins of the fan headers are J7 pin 2 and J8 pin 2 (\"the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)\"); pin 2 should therefore sit on the +12V net, with the tach signal on the remaining signal pin (standard 4-pin fan wiring: 1=GND, 2=+12V, 3=Tach, 4=PWM/NC).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Also on this net: ... and the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet: J7 Conn_01x04_Pin with FAN_0_TACH on pin 2 and +12V on pin 1; J8 Conn_01x04_Pin with FAN_1_TACH on pin 2 and +12V on pin 1"}
      ],
      "impact": "The fans would not receive +12V on the pin the design expects; instead a logic-level tach signal is presented on the power pin and +12V is applied to a different pin, so the fans would not run correctly and the tach inputs would be mis-driven (potential damage to the fan and/or the ESP32 tach input).",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Most of the board matches the specification (ESP32-C3, three MAX31855 channels with shared SCK/SO and separate CS0-2, TPS2121 power mux, AP63203 buck, TPS1H200 heater switch, USB-C and WS2812B), but the fan headers J7/J8 appear to have the FAN_0_TACH/FAN_1_TACH signals on pin 2, the pin the spec designates as the +12V fan supply, so the fan interface is mis-wired."
}

